 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{261.783}{431.999}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 federates in the federation execution to the state in which they were when the save was accomplished. The\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{273.783}{431.999}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 federation can then resume effecting the execution from that saved point. Once a restore has begun, any\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{285.782}{431.999}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 messages that have not been delivered to the intended joined federates in the restoring federation execution\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{297.782}{431.999}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 shall be lost. After a restore is complete, the RTI shall deliver all related saved messages as appropriate to\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{309.782}{431.999}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 the joined federates in the restored federation execution (e.g., TSO messages at the federate’s appropriate\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{321.782}{315.067}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 logical time position or RO messages as soon as possible in wall-clock time).%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{345.781}{431.999}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The set of federates joined to a federation execution when state is restored from a previously saved state\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.002}{357.781}{431.998}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 need not be the exact set of federates that were joined to the federation execution when the state being\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{369.781}{431.997}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 restored was saved. The number of federates of each federate type that are joined to the federation execution,\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{381.781}{431.997}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 however, shall be the same. The federate-type argument supplied in the \emph{Join Federation Execution} service\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{393.781}{431.997}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 invocation, therefore, is crucial to the save-and-restore process. Declaring a federate to be of a given type\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{405.780}{431.997}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 shall be equivalent to asserting that the joined federate can be restored using the state information saved by\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{417.780}{156.191}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 any other joined federate of that type.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{441.780}{431.997}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 There is no requirement that a save taken by one RTI implementation be restorable by another RTI\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{453.780}{71.266}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 implementation.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{477.640}{163.761}{\sffamily\bfseries\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 \textbf{4.1.3 Synchronization discussion}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{501.779}{431.997}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Figure 6 depicts the state of a federation execution with respect to synchronization points. In order to fully\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{513.779}{431.997}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 explain synchronization points, both the state of the RTI and the state of each joined federate are detailed.\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{525.779}{431.997}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Because multiple joined federates are involved, each transition within this set of state charts includes an\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{537.778}{431.997}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 argument denoting to which joined federate a service invocation applies (i.e., an argument of \emph{F}\raisebox{-2.2pt}{\fontsize{7.98pt}{8pt}\selectfont \emph{s}} denotes the\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{549.785}{85.728}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 \emph{s}th joined federate).%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{573.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The RTI shall keep track of each synchronization point separately, and the treatment of an arbitrary\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{585.785}{432.001}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 synchronization point by the RTI is expanded in Figure 7. Not shown in this diagram is how the RTI\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{597.785}{432.001}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 calculates the synchronization set (shown as \emph{synch\_set} within the state chart); this information is detailed in\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{609.784}{432.002}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 the service descriptions. Use of the synchronization set, however, is depicted in the state chart on the\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{621.784}{432.002}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 transition from the Synchronizing (s, L) states to the Synch Point Not Registered state. This transition\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{633.784}{432.002}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 denotes, by use of the universal quantifier over the synchronization set in the guard, that synchronization\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{645.784}{432.002}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 throughout a federation is achieved only when all joined federates in the synchronization set have concluded\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{657.784}{432.002}{\rmfamily}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 their individual synchronizations. A joined federate’s individual synchronization, from the RTI’s point of\linebreak%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{669.783}{122.633}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 view, is detailed in Figure 8.%
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
