 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{67.530}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 LITS is more useful for time-constrained joined federates, but it applies to all joined federates{\fontsize{7.98pt}{9.48pt}\selectfont 18}. LITS is%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{81.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 useful for joined federates wishing to know the timestamp of the next TSO message that they may have to%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{93.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 process.%
 }
 \HLAServiceComponentHeadingAt{117.645}{%
 \textbf{8.1.7 Putting it all together}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{141.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The state chart shown in Figure 17 illustrates when a joined federate may become time-regulating and%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{153.784}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 time-constrained, when time advances may be requested, how a joined federate enables or disables%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{165.784}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 asynchronous message delivery, the effect these activities have on determining sent and received message%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{177.784}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 order types, and when messages may be sent and received.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{201.784}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The transition labeled “Send Message” shall represent any service invocation that is called sending a%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{213.783}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 message. As represented in the state chart, such a transition can occur in any state and shall result in the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{225.783}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 joined federate returning to whatever state it was in before the transition. The column to the right of the state%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{237.783}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 chart elaborates on how the order type of the sent message is determined. Each part of the definition of%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{249.783}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 “Send Message” shall be composed of a conversion rule (denoted as two terms separated by an arrow) and%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{261.783}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 an optional boolean guard (denoted in square braces, just as in state charts). The term to the left of the arrow%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{273.783}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 in each conversion rule represents the preferred order type of the message and whether a timestamp was%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{285.782}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 provided by the invoking joined federate. The term to the right of the arrow represents the order type of the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{297.782}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 sent message. The guard represents under what circumstances the conversion rule applies. Therefore, each%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{309.782}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 part of the definition reads as follows: if the preferred order type of the message is as indicated to the left of%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{321.782}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 the arrow, the usage of a timestamp is as described to the left of the arrow, and the boolean guard (if present)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{333.782}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 is true, then the order type of the sent message is as indicated to the right of the arrow. The conversion rules%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{345.781}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 provided in the state chart are the same as the results contained in Table 2 and Table 3.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{369.781}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The transitions labeled “Receive Message \#1” and “Receive Message \#2” read similarly with one exception:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{381.781}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The conversion rules are slightly different. The term to the left of the arrow represents the order type of the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{393.781}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 received message. The term to the right of the arrow represents the order type of the corresponding sent%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{405.780}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 message.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{429.780}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 A joined federate may send messages while in any state in this diagram. If the joined federate is%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{441.780}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 time-regulating and sending a TSO message, the timestamp of that message shall be constrained as%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{453.780}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 described in 8.1.5 with one exception: When a joined federate is in the Time Advancing state, the timestamp%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{465.780}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 of sent TSO messages is constrained by the joined federate’s requested logical time (plus its lookahead)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{477.779}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 rather than by the joined federate’s current logical time (plus its lookahead). If the joined federate is granted%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{489.779}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 to a logical time that is less than its requested logical time (i.e., the request used the \emph{Next Message Request},%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{501.779}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 \emph{Next Message Request Available}, or \emph{Flush Queue Request} service), the constraints shall ease upon leaving%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{513.779}{432.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 the Time Advancing state. The constraints on advancing and nonadvancing joined federates are summarized%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{525.779}{432.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 in Table 1.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{549.778}{432.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 When joined federates are eligible to receive messages is dependent on several factors. If the joined federate%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{561.778}{432.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 is not time-constrained, it may receive messages while in any state (although only RO messages may be%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{573.778}{432.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 received). If the joined federate is time-constrained, it shall normally receive messages only when in the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{585.778}{432.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Time Advancing state. However, joined federates may enable asynchronous message delivery (via the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{597.777}{432.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 \emph{Enable Asynchronous Delivery} service), which permits them to receive RO messages (but not TSO%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{609.777}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 messages) when not in the Time Advancing state.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{633.777}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Which RO messages will be received when a joined federate is eligible to receive RO messages shall depend%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{645.777}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 only on which messages have been sent that will be received as RO messages by that joined federate. In%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{657.776}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 general, if a joined federate is eligible to receive RO messages, it may receive all RO messages that it has not%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{669.776}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 yet received.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{696.453}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{6.36}{12.00}{%
 18{\fontsize{7.98pt}{9.48pt}\selectfont In the case of a nonconstrained joined federate, messages that may be received by that joined federate and that have a sent message}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{707.250}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{7.98}{12.00}{%
 order type of TSO may or may not be included in the calculation of the joined federate’s LITS.%
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
\clearpage

\HLASourcePage{186}{185}
\HLAServiceBookmark{8.1.6}{2}{Lookahead}
\thispagestyle{hla-body}
\begin{hlafixedpage}
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{69.786}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Which TSO messages will be received when a joined federate is eligible to receive TSO messages shall%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{81.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 depend on which TSO messages have been sent that will be received as TSO messages, what timestamps the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{93.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 messages have, and what form of time advancement was requested. Precisely which TSO messages will be%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{105.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 received shall be defined in each of the different time advancement services.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{129.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Because messages are not always eligible for delivery, the RTI shall internally queue pending messages for%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{141.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 each joined federate. The RTI shall queue all messages that the joined federate will receive as TSO or RO%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{153.784}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 messages. When messages are finally delivered to the joined federate, they shall be removed from the queue.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{177.784}{431.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 %
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
\clearpage

