 \HLAServiceComponentHeadingAt{69.646}{%
 \textbf{8.1.8 Delay Subscription Evaluation Switch}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{93.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The federation execution-wide Delay Subscription Evaluation Switch indicates how the RTI shall filter a%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{105.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 TSO or RO message based on the receiving joined federate’s current subscriptions. The setting for this%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{117.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 switch shall come from the FDD at federation execution creation time. If no value is specified for this%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{129.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 switch, then the RTI will provide default values based on the switches table defaults table in IEEE Std%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{141.785}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 1516.2-2025. This switch setting shall not be modifiable during a federation execution.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{165.784}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Whenever this switch is enabled, the RTI shall delay subscription evaluation of a message until the message%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{177.784}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 is eligible for delivery to the joined federate.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{201.784}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Whenever this switch is disabled, the RTI may determine if a message is eligible for delivery to a joined%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{213.783}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 federate based on the current subscriptions of that joined federate when the message is generated. If this%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{225.783}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 immediate subscription evaluation indicates that the message is not eligible for delivery to the joined%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{237.783}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 federate, then that message shall never be eligible for delivery to the joined federate regardless of any%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{249.783}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 changes to subscriptions (by that joined federate) that occur subsequent to the message generation.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{273.782}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 In both cases, when a message is eligible for delivery to a joined federate, the RTI shall use the federate’s%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{285.782}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 current subscriptions to determine if it will be actually be delivered to the joined federate.%
 }
 \HLAServiceComponentHeadingAt{309.642}{%
 \textbf{8.1.9 Warnings on partial use of time management}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{333.781}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 When there are some joined federates in a federation execution that use time management services and other%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{345.781}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 joined federates that do not, there are some complications of which federation developers should be aware.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{357.781}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The complications stem from the fact that some joined federates are making use of TSO ordering of%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{369.781}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 messages, while others are accepting RO ordering of these same messages. The two basic scenarios (which%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{381.781}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 can, of course, occur independently or together in the same federation execution) are as follows:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{393.781}{421.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 a)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.981}{393.781}{400.019}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 A time-regulating joined federate sends TSO messages that are received by a nonconstrained joined%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{405.780}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 federate in RO.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{417.780}{421.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 b)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.937}{417.780}{400.063}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 A nonregulating joined federate sends RO messages to a joined federate that expects to receive%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{429.780}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 messages in TSO.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{453.780}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The former tends to be more of a problem because, among other reasons, the time-regulating joined federate%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{465.779}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 need not send its messages in TSO. They may (for example) be received by the nonconstrained joined%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{477.779}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 federate in the same order as sent, which is not equivalent to the order in which the sender meant them to be%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{489.779}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 received.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{513.779}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The latter is not as much of a problem because the nonregulating joined federate will send its messages in%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{525.778}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 the order it means for them to be received. The time-constrained joined federate will receive them in this%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{537.778}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 order (barring factors like differing transportation types among the sent messages).%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{561.778}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Another factor to consider is that joined federates do not need to be time-regulating with respect to all%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{573.778}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 messages. A time-regulating joined federate can send some messages TSO and send other messages RO.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{585.777}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 This situation can also lead to complications if the decision on order type is not carefully considered.%
 }
 \HLAServiceComponentHeadingAt{609.638}{%
 \textbf{8.1.10 Mixing use of TSO and RO messages}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{633.777}{432.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Some specific examples of odd results when mixing the use of TSO and RO messages are presented below:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{645.777}{421.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 a)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.968}{645.777}{400.032}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 A time-regulating joined federate invokes the \emph{Delete Object Instance} service and provides a%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{657.776}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 timestamp far in its future (much greater than its logical time). Subsequently, it sends several update%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{669.776}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 messages, modeling attributes of the object instance in the interval prior to its deletion and providing%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{681.776}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 a timestamp to each update that is less than the timestamp of the deletion. Nonconstrained joined%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{693.776}{400.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 federates may receive these messages in the order in which they were sent. In other words, a%
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
\clearpage

\HLASourcePage{189}{188}
\HLAServiceBookmark{8.1.11}{2}{Order types}
\HLAServiceBookmark{8.2}{1}{Enable Time Regulation service}
\thispagestyle{hla-body}
\begin{hlafixedpage}
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{69.786}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 nonconstrained joined federate may receive the delete message and never receive any of the update%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{81.785}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 messages.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{93.785}{421.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 b)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{93.785}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 A time-regulating joined federate invokes the \emph{Delete Object Instance} service and provides a%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.981}{105.785}{400.019}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 timestamp. This TSO message will be received as RO by any joined federates that are not%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{117.785}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 time-constrained. This situation may result in nonconstrained joined federates receiving this%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{129.785}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 message much sooner in wall-clock time than time-constrained joined federates if the timestamp%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{141.785}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 provided with the delete was greater than the logical time of most time-constrained joined federates.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{153.784}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Even more problematic is if the nonconstrained joined federate later decides to become time-%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{165.784}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 constrained. If it becomes time-constrained at a federation time less than the timestamp of the delete,%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{177.784}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 it may now discover the object instance for which it just received a delete message. However, any%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{189.784}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 messages regarding this object instance may have been dequeued for this joined federate when it%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{201.784}{400.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 received the delete message, and subsequent behavior may not be what is expected.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{213.783}{421.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 c)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.969}{213.783}{400.031}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 A time-regulating joined federate that is sending both TSO and RO messages can in fact send both%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{225.783}{400.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 kinds of messages in a single \emph{Update Attribute Values} service invocation. The message retraction%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{237.783}{400.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 designator that is returned on this service invocation, however, applies only to the resulting sent%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{249.783}{400.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 TSO messages, not to the whole service invocation. Consequently, any instance attribute values that%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.978}{261.783}{400.022}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 resulted in sent RO messages shall not be retracted by invocations of the \emph{Retract} service that include%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.978}{273.783}{400.022}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 the returned message retraction designator.%
 }
 \HLAServiceComponentHeadingAt{297.643}{%
 \textbf{8.1.11 Order types}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{321.782}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The following order types shall be the only ones supported by the RTI:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.018}{336.781}{421.982}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 a)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.976}{336.781}{400.024}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 TSO: (legal name shall be “TimeStamp”)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.018}{351.781}{421.982}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 b)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.941}{351.781}{400.059}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 RO: (legal name shall be “Receive”)%
 }
 \HLAServiceHeadingAt{381.383}{%
 \textbf{8.2 }\textbf{\emph{Enable Time Regulation}}\textbf{ service}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{406.806}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 The \emph{Enable Time Regulation} service shall enable time-regulation for the joined federate invoking the service%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{418.805}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 and thereby enable the joined federate to send TSO messages. The joined federate shall request that its%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{430.805}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 lookahead be set to the specified value. The RTI shall indicate the logical time assigned to the joined%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{442.805}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 federate through the \emph{Time Regulation Enabled \textdagger{}} service. The logical time provided when time-regulation is%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{454.805}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 enabled shall be the smallest possible logical time that is greater than or equal to the joined federate’s current%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{466.805}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 logical time and for which all other constraints necessary to ensure TSO message delivery are satisfied. In%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{478.805}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 other words, in general, the logical time that the joined federate will be given (plus the requested lookahead)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{490.804}{432.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 will always be greater than or equal to the maximum logical time of all time-constrained joined federates%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{502.804}{432.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 (with the possibility of being of equal value depending on what form of time advancement was used by each%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{514.804}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 time-constrained joined federate). %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{538.804}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Upon the RTI’s invocation of the corresponding \emph{Time Regulation Enabled \textdagger{}} service, the invoking joined%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{550.803}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 federate may begin sending TSO messages that have a timestamp greater than or equal to the joined%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{562.803}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 federate’s logical time plus the joined federate’s lookahead. Zero lookahead joined federates are not subject%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{574.803}{432.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 to additional restrictions when time-regulation is first enabled.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{598.803}{432.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 Because the invocation of this service may require the RTI to advance the invoking joined federate’s logical%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{610.802}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 time, this service has an additional meaning for time-constrained joined federates. Since the advancing%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{622.802}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 logical time for a time-constrained joined federate is synonymous with a guarantee that all TSO messages%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{634.802}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 with timestamps less than the new logical time have been delivered, the invocation of this service shall be%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{646.802}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 considered an implicit \emph{Time Advance Request Available} service invocation. The subsequent invocation of%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{658.802}{432.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 \emph{Time Regulation Enabled \textdagger{}} service shall be considered an implicit \emph{Time Advance Grant \textdagger{}} service%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.997}{670.801}{432.003}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 invocation. Thus, if a time-constrained joined federate attempts to become time-regulating, it may receive%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.997}{682.801}{432.003}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 RO and TSO messages between its invocation of the \emph{Enable Time Regulation} service and the RTI’s%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.997}{694.801}{432.003}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 invocation of the \emph{Time Regulation Enabled \textdagger{}} service at the joined federate. This special case is not%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.997}{706.801}{432.003}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{12.00}{%
 illustrated in the state chart in Figure 17.%
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
