 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{219.383}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{12.9 Dynamic link compatibility}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{244.806}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The Application Programmer Interfaces (API) defined in this specification were designed to meet the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{256.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 purpose and objectives for dynamic link compatibility (DLC) of RTIs. In general, DLC is the ability to%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{268.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 compile federate code that uses the RTI API such that RTI implementation libraries can be switchable at run-%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{280.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 time without having to recompile the federate code. The precise details of dynamic link compatibility are%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{292.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 language specific and are described in detail in the appropriate language sections.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{322.403}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{\emph{12.10 Callback representation of timestamps, order type, retraction handle and }}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{335.423}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{\emph{regions}}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{360.786}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The use of a timestamp in some object management services determines which parameters are represented in%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{372.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 the corresponding callback(s). When a timestamp is omitted when calling the %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{420.300}{372.785}{183.700}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Update Attribute Values}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{519.479}{372.785}{84.521}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ,%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{384.785}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Delete Object Instance}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{181.739}{384.785}{422.261}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 , %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{186.838}{384.785}{417.162}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Send Interaction}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{253.258}{384.785}{350.742}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 or %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{266.758}{384.785}{337.242}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Send Directed Interaction}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{370.378}{384.785}{233.622}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 services, the corresponding callbacks%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{396.785}{514.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 also omit a timestamp parameter, both sent and receive order parameters, as well as a retraction handle%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{408.785}{514.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 parameter. When the federate makes these service calls without a timestamp, there is no timestamp to%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{420.785}{514.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 deliver, the sent and receive order are always receive order and only TSO messages can be retracted. As a%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{432.785}{514.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 result, the APIs have one corresponding callback signature with these parameters and one without. When%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{444.784}{514.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 present in the signature, the retraction handle is provided as a reference (pointer in C++). The retraction%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{456.784}{514.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 handle parameter may have no value, indicated by a null reference, when the receive order is not TSO.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{468.784}{514.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Similarly, if the signature includes a sent regions parameter, it is also provided as a reference that may have%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{480.784}{514.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 no value (no available dimensions or convey region designator switch is disabled, see 9.1.7).%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{510.383}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{12.11 Java}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{535.806}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The Java API shall be based on Java 11. The Java API is composed of interfaces and classes. Where an%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{547.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 interface is specified, the RTI implementers shall furnish an implementation of the interface. For instance,%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{560.393}{513.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{RTIambassador}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{167.941}{559.805}{436.059}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 is specified as an interface. Therefore, an RTI implementer shall furnish an%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{571.805}{513.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 implementation of the interface, for instance:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{596.392}{513.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{package com.myrticompany.amazingrti; }%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{608.392}{513.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{public final class MyRTIimpl implements hla.rti1516\_2025.RTIambassador }%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{620.392}{513.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{\{\textbackslash{}ldots\{\}\}}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{643.804}{513.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Each such interface as specified remains part of the API. Thus, federate code can use the specified interface%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{655.804}{513.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 type. The %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{131.401}{656.391}{472.599}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{FederateAmbassador}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{239.341}{655.804}{364.659}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 , however, is an exception to this rule and shall be implemented by a%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{667.803}{513.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
