 \HLAFixedBlock{90.005}{309.782}{513.995}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Subclause 1.4.4 in IEEE Std 1516.2-2025 presents general conventions used to describe the abstract data%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.005}{321.782}{513.995}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 types used in Clause 4 through Clause 10. In 12.12.6.1 through 12.12.6.26, the abstract data types are further%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.005}{333.782}{513.995}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 detailed by mapping an individual data type to its implementation in the C++ API. Note that the data types%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.005}{345.781}{513.995}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 from Clause 11 are explained in that clause and are not repeated here. Additionally, designators are a subject%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.005}{357.781}{513.995}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 in their own right and are covered in 12.2.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.005}{381.781}{513.995}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The ISO/IEC committee ratified the C++ standard as ISO/IEC 14882 [B8]. The HLA C++ API utilizes%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.005}{393.781}{513.995}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 standard C++ constructs to the fullest extent possible. For example, %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{368.225}{394.368}{235.775}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{std::wstring}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{440.224}{393.781}{163.776}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 is used to represent%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.004}{405.780}{513.996}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 character strings that might previously have been represented with the C-style construct, pointer to %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{498.004}{406.368}{105.996}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{char}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.004}{417.780}{513.996}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 (i.e.,%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{109.980}{418.373}{494.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{char *}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{145.800}{417.786}{458.200}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ). Additionally, several standard C++ containers are used to represent the sets, constrained sets%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{429.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 of pairs, and collections described in Clause 3. These containers are %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{365.280}{430.373}{238.720}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{std::set<T>}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{431.220}{429.785}{172.780}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 , %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{436.440}{430.373}{167.560}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{std::map<T>}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{502.380}{429.785}{101.620}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 , and%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{442.373}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{std::vector<T>}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{173.940}{441.785}{430.060}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 . Also, the C++ standard %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{289.560}{442.373}{314.440}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{std::unique\_ptr<T>}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{397.500}{441.785}{206.500}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 is used to implement strict%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{453.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 memory ownership transfer when passing instances of dynamically allocated data types across API.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{477.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 A description of the nature and properties of these standard C++ constructs is well beyond the scope of this%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{489.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 clause. Instead, readers are referred to the C++ ISO standard as well as any of the vast assortment of C++%
 }
