\HLASourcePage{398}{397}
\HLAServiceBookmark{12.13.4}{2}{Session Layer}
\HLAServiceBookmark{12.13.4.1}{3}{Message Types and Message Header Overview}
\thispagestyle{hla-body}
\begin{hlafixedpage}
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{69.786}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 f)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{69.786}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Logical time, timestamps and lookahead}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{303.000}{69.786}{301.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 : Logical time, timestamps, and lookahead shall be%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{81.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 encoded according to the encoding format defined for the time type.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{93.785}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 g)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{93.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Optional arguments}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{207.780}{93.785}{396.220}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 : For HLA services that have optional arguments, a single service is mapped to%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{105.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 multiple HLA service messages. All fields in the HLA service messages are mandatory and values%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{117.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 shall be provided when a service is invoked, except where explicitly stated otherwise.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{129.785}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 h)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{129.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Maps and Sets}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{184.920}{129.785}{419.080}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 : The Annex D files define several data types that have names that end with “Map”%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{141.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 and “Set.” A Map is an object that maps keys to values. A Map shall not have duplicate keys, and%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{153.784}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 each key shall only map to at most one value. A Set is a collection that shall not contain duplicates.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{165.784}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 These semantics are not enforced by the Annex D encoding data types but they shall be maintained%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{177.784}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 by the user.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{189.784}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 i)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.951}{189.784}{482.049}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Exceptions: The Annex E files define the encoding of exceptions as the exception name and its%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{201.784}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 details as native strings. The exception name string shall be the string returned by the Exception data%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{213.783}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 type method called “name” as specified in12.11.6.3 and 12.12.6.3.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{237.644}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{12.13.4 Session Layer}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{261.783}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The session layer provides a fault tolerant session between the federate and RTI on top of the transport layer.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{273.783}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Fault tolerance enables a federate session to survive temporary disconnections in the transport layer%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{285.782}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 connection.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{309.782}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The session layer supports the following:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{333.782}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ---%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{333.782}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Session establishment and termination;%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{345.781}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ---%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{345.781}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 HLA service calls initiated from the federate;%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{357.781}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ---%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{357.781}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 HLA service callbacks initiated from the RTI;%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{369.781}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ---%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{369.781}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Resumption of sessions after temporary disconnect.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{393.641}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{12.13.4.1 Message Types and Message Header Overview}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{417.780}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 A message consists of a header and a payload. Three key fields of the header are:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{441.780}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ---%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{441.780}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Session ID, which contains an identifier of the federate session. The RTI shall provide a unique ID%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{453.780}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 for all sessions. The value zero shall be used to indicate “no session.”%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{465.779}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ---%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{465.779}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Sequence number, which contains the sequence number of each message. The federate and the RTI%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{477.779}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 shall maintain their own sequence numbers as shown in Figure 22. The sequence number is used for%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{489.779}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 matching requests with responses as shown in Figure 23. %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{501.779}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ---%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{501.779}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Message type, which indicates the type of message, as described below.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{525.778}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 There are four types of messages used for HLA services and eight types of control messages as follows:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{549.778}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 a)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.977}{549.778}{482.023}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 HLA\_CALL\_REQUEST, sent from the federate to perform an HLA service invocation. The%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{561.778}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 payload of the message contains the HLA service and its arguments.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{573.778}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 b)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{573.778}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 HLA\_CALL\_RESPONSE, sent from the RTI to provide return values or exceptions thrown in%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{585.777}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 response to an HLA service invocation. This message includes an “In response to (IRT)” field,%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{597.777}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 indicating to which HLA\_CALL\_REQUEST this is a response.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{609.777}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 c)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.978}{609.777}{482.022}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 HLA\_CALLBACK\_REQUEST, sent from the RTI to indicate an HLA callback invocation. The%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{621.777}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 payload of the message contains the HLA service and its arguments.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{633.777}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 d)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{633.777}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 HLA\_CALLBACK\_RESPONSE, sent from the federate to provide return values or exceptions%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.981}{645.776}{482.019}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 thrown in response to an HLA callback invocation. This message includes an IRT field, indicating to%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.982}{657.776}{482.018}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 which HLA\_CALLBACK\_REQUEST this is a response.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.022}{669.776}{503.978}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 e)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.981}{669.776}{482.019}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 CONTROL, sent from either the federate or RTI to manage the session and connection. There are%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.982}{681.776}{482.018}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 eight types of Control messages:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.982}{693.776}{482.018}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 1)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.030}{693.776}{461.970}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 CTRL\_NEW\_SESSION to request a new session%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.982}{705.776}{482.018}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 2)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.031}{705.776}{461.969}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 CTRL\_NEW\_SESSION\_STATUS to return the status of the new session request%
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
\clearpage

\HLASourcePage{399}{398}
\HLAServiceBookmark{12.13.4.2}{3}{Principles for exchanging messages}
\HLAServiceBookmark{12.13.4.3}{3}{Setting up and Terminating a Session}
\thispagestyle{hla-body}
\begin{hlafixedpage}
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{78.786}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 3)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.051}{78.786}{461.949}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 CTRL\_TERMINATE\_SESSION to terminate a session%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{90.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 4)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.027}{90.785}{461.973}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 CTRL\_SESSION\_TERMINATED to confirm the above%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{102.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 5)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.028}{102.785}{461.972}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 CTRL\_HEARTBEAT to maintain a connection%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{114.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 6)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.032}{114.785}{461.968}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 CTRL\_HEARTBEAT\_RESPONSE to respond to the heartbeat%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{126.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 7)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.027}{126.785}{461.973}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 CTRL\_RESUME\_REQUEST to request the resumption of a session after an interruption%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{138.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 8)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.028}{138.785}{461.972}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 CTRL\_RESUME\_STATUS to return the status of the resume request%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{162.784}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 These messages are described in detail in section 12.13.4.5.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{186.645}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{12.13.4.2 Principles for exchanging messages}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{210.784}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The session layer enables the federate and RTI to exchange messages with each other. Messages can be sent%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{222.783}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 in both directions independently as shown in Figure 22. %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{388.443}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 All messages contain a header field with sequence numbers. Each side maintains its own sequence%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{400.443}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 numbering, labeled in Figure 22 as F1..F4 for federate sequence numbers and R1..R4 for RTI sequence%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{412.443}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 numbers. Several message types provide an IRT number, indicating the message number to which this%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{424.443}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 message is a response, as shown in Figure 23.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{588.662}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 In Figure 23, the R3 message is a response to the F2 message, and R4 is a response to F3. %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{612.522}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{12.13.4.3 Setting up and Terminating a Session}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{636.661}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The life cycle of a session is described below. It is assumed that a connection, using the transport layer, has%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{648.661}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 already been established. Additional information about fault tolerance is provided in 12.13.4.4.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{672.661}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 a)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{672.661}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Session Initiation}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{684.660}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The federate initiates a new federate session with the RTI using the New Session control message. %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{696.660}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 When a successful session has been established, the RTI shall return a session ID using the New %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{708.660}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Session Status control message.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{164.700}{361.066}{439.300}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Figure 22---Federate and RTI message sequence numbers}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{234.180}{565.966}{369.820}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Figure 23---Response messages}%
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
\clearpage

\HLASourcePage{400}{399}
\HLAServiceBookmark{12.13.4.4}{3}{Fault Tolerance}
\thispagestyle{hla-body}
\begin{hlafixedpage}
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{69.786}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 b)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{69.786}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{HLA Service Exchange}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{81.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Messages, such as HLA Call Request/Response and HLA Callback Request/Response can then be %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{93.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 exchanged between the federate and the RTI.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{105.785}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 c)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{105.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Heartbeating}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{117.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Should the federate not need to send any messages for some time, the federate shall regularly send a %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{129.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Heartbeat message to maintain the connection. The heartbeat interval is described in Table 32. The %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{141.785}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 RTI shall respond to this message by sending a Heartbeat Response message.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{153.784}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 d)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{153.784}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Fault Tolerance: Reconnecting}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{165.784}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 If a connection fault occurs (see 12.13.4.4), the federate may try to reconnect and resume a federate %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{177.784}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 session by sending a Resume Request message. The RTI shall respond with a Resume Status %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{189.784}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 message. Further details are provided in section 12.13.4.4.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{201.784}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 e)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{201.784}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Session Termination}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{213.783}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 When a federate no longer wishes to be connected to the RTI, it shall initiate a termination sequence %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{225.783}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 using the Terminate Session control message. Once the session has been finalized at the RTI, the %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{237.783}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 session ID is no longer valid. The RTI shall respond with a Session Terminated control message and %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{249.783}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 purge the session. The federate shall wait for this message and then close the transport layer %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{261.783}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 connection.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{285.643}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{12.13.4.4 Fault Tolerance}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{309.782}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 To support reconnection after a connection fault has occurred in the connection layer, the federate and the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{321.782}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 RTI shall maintain a message history buffer from which lost messages can be resent. The following%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{333.782}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 conditions are considered connection faults that may be recoverable:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{357.781}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ---%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{357.781}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The socket connection is broken%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{369.781}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ---%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{369.781}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The FED\_TIMEOUT\_HEART or RTI\_TIMEOUT\_HEART was exceeded%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{393.781}{514.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 If the RTI or federate receives a bad message (see 12.13.4.7), the session shall enter a fatal state and cannot%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.998}{405.780}{514.002}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 be recovered. A federate may attempt recovery if the RTI detects the bad message and closes the socket, but%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.997}{417.780}{514.003}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 the recovery shall fail.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.997}{441.780}{514.003}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Figure 24 shows an overview of the states of a federate. A more detailed state diagram with additional%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.997}{453.780}{514.003}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 exception handling is provided in section 12.13.4.7.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.997}{685.139}{514.003}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The federate starts by connecting to the RTI and initiating a new session. Once a valid session is available,%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.997}{697.139}{514.003}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 HLA service calls and callbacks can be exchanged. Finally, the federate initiates the termination of the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{173.400}{645.886}{430.600}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Figure 24---Federate Connection and Reconnection States}%
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
\clearpage

\HLASourcePage{401}{400}
\thispagestyle{hla-body}
\begin{hlafixedpage}
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{69.786}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 session. If a connection fault occurs at the federate, the federate may enter a state where it tries to reconnect.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{81.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 If such a reconnection can be established, the federate may attempt to resume the session. The RTI shall then%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{93.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 resend lost messages. The federate should also resend lost messages. Control messages shall be ignored;%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{105.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 only HLA service messages shall be resent. If all lost messages are successfully exchanged, then the RTI and%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{117.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 the federate can continue exchanging new messages. If all lost messages cannot be exchanged, the session%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{129.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 cannot be resumed and is no longer valid.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{153.784}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Figure 25 shows an overview of the states of the RTI for a given federate session. A more detailed state%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{165.784}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 diagram with additional exception handling is provided in section 12.13.4.7.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{379.804}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The RTI starts by waiting for a federate to connect and initiate a new session. Once a valid session is%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{391.803}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 available, HLA service calls and callbacks can be exchanged. Finally, when the federate initiates the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{403.803}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 termination of the session, the RTI terminates the corresponding session. If a connection fault occurs at the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{415.803}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 RTI, the RTI shall enter a state where it waits for a reconnection. If such a reconnection can be established%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{427.803}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 and the federate attempts to resume the session, the RTI shall resend lost messages. If all lost messages are%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{439.803}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 successfully exchanged, then the RTI and the federate can continue exchanging new messages. If not all lost%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{451.803}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 messages can be exchanged, the session cannot be resumed and is no longer valid.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{475.802}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The RTI shall also purge sessions that have been inactive for a period of time, according to Table 31. This%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{487.802}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 purge shall include resigning these federates from any federation that they may be joined to, using the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{499.802}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 automatic resign directive.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{523.801}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The steps of a reconnection are shown in Figure 26.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{181.740}{352.846}{422.260}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Figure 25---RTI Connection and Reconnection States}%
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
\clearpage

\HLASourcePage{402}{401}
\thispagestyle{hla-body}
\begin{hlafixedpage}
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{297.186}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The overall steps are:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{321.185}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 a)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{321.185}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The federate and the RTI evaluate whether it is possible to resume.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{333.185}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 b)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{333.185}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 HLA service messages that were lost in transit are resent by the federate and the RTI in their original%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{345.185}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 order, using their original sequence numbers. Control messages are ignored; only HLA service%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.980}{357.185}{482.020}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 messages are resent.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.020}{369.185}{503.980}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 c)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.983}{369.185}{482.017}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 New messages can then be exchanged. %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{393.184}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The steps for evaluating whether a session can be resumed without losing messages are as follows:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.021}{417.184}{503.979}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 a)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.983}{417.184}{482.017}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The federate shall determine the sequence number of its Last Received RTI (LRR) message, as well%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.982}{429.184}{482.018}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 as the sequence number of the Oldest Available Federate (OAF) message in its message history%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.982}{441.183}{482.018}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 buffer. %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.022}{453.183}{503.978}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 b)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.944}{453.183}{482.056}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The sequence numbers of these messages shall be sent by the federate to the RTI using the Resume%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.982}{465.183}{482.018}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Request message.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.022}{477.183}{503.978}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 c)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.982}{477.183}{482.018}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Upon receipt of this message, the RTI shall determine the sequence number of its Last Received%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.983}{489.183}{482.017}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Federate (LRF) message, as well as the sequence number of the Oldest Available RTI (OAR)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.983}{501.182}{482.017}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 message in its message history buffer.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.023}{513.182}{503.977}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 d)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.983}{513.182}{482.017}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The RTI shall determine if it is possible to resend all lost messages by making the following check:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.983}{525.182}{482.017}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 1)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.035}{525.182}{461.965}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 That all federate messages not received by the RTI are available in the federate message history%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.023}{537.182}{461.977}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 buffer (LRF is not older than OAF) and%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.984}{549.182}{482.016}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 2)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.040}{549.182}{461.960}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 All RTI messages not received by the federate are available in the RTI message history buffer%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{142.023}{561.181}{461.977}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 (LRR is not older than OAR).%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.023}{573.241}{503.977}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 e)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.982}{573.241}{482.018}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The RTI shall respond with a Resume Status message indicating success or failure.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{597.241}{513.997}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 When the RTI sends a message to a federate, it shall provide the sequence number of the last message it has%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{609.240}{513.997}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 received from the federate in the Last Received Message (LRM) field of the message header. A federate%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{621.240}{513.997}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 receiving a message from the RTI can then use the LRM value to discard messages from its sent message%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{633.240}{513.997}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 buffer which are equal to or older than the LRM value as they have already been received by the RTI.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{645.240}{513.997}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Similarly, a federate shall send LRM values to the RTI and the RTI can use them to discard messages from%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{657.240}{513.997}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 the sent message buffer for that federate. In this way, the RTI and federate sent message buffers can be%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{669.239}{513.997}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 maintained with just the messages that are needed to resume a session should a failure occur.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.003}{693.239}{513.997}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Only HLA service messages shall be considered when determining the last received or oldest available%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.002}{705.239}{513.998}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 message sequence number excluding any control messages (e.g., Resume Request and Resume Status).%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{238.320}{254.986}{365.680}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{Figure 26---Successful reconnection}%
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
