 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{68.648}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{13.98}{15.66}{%
 \textbf{Annex E}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{97.140}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{12.00}{13.44}{%
 (normative) %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{128.648}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{13.98}{15.66}{%
 \textbf{Rationale}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{169.140}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{12.00}{13.44}{%
 \textbf{E.1 Overview}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{195.786}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 This annex contains additional information that might be useful to explain various aspects of HLA and is not%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{207.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 a complete rationale of the HLA. Nothing in this clause is required in an HLA implementation.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{231.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 This annex is organized to match the organization of the main body of this specification, and the subclause%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{243.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 numbers in this annex correspond to the subclause numbers of the main body. Note, however, that not all%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{255.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 subclauses in the main body have corresponding subclauses in this annex. In other words, text is present in%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{267.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 this annex only if additional comments were deemed helpful to understanding the corresponding subclauses%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{279.784}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 in the main body.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{309.383}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{E.1.4 Background}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{334.806}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Consideration was given to providing several iterating functions; however, there were found to be serious%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{346.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 problems documenting and describing the iterating-based semantics of these services. Additionally, there%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{358.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 was a problem with every redaction produced. The candidate definitions of how exceptions were handled%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{370.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 and recovered from during the “middle” of an iteration sequence never satisfied a large enough set of users%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{382.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 to be acceptable for retention in the specification. Each of the users wanted the behavior to meet their%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{394.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 specific application needs, which in most cases were inconsistent and impossible to meet with a common%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{406.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 mechanism. As a result, it seemed to make the most sense not to have any iterating services and to let users%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{418.804}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 produce their own aggregate functionality with the exception response semantics they needed.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{454.140}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{12.00}{13.44}{%
 \textbf{E.4 Federation management}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{486.383}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{E.4.6 }%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{118.680}{486.383}{485.320}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{\emph{Destroy Federation Execution}}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{274.379}{486.383}{329.621}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{ service}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{511.806}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Since there can be no joined federates for the destroy service invocation to succeed, there might be a%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{523.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 problem if the federation execution has “zombie” or otherwise uncooperative joined federates. To aid%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{535.805}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 matters in this situation, a joined federate can be resigned by another joined federate via a MOM interaction.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{565.403}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{E.4.31 }%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{124.800}{565.403}{479.200}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{\emph{Query Federation Restore Status}}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{296.341}{565.403}{307.659}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{ service}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{590.826}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 One way to employ federation restore status is for a federation manager federate to not invoke the %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{491.820}{590.826}{112.180}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Restore}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{602.825}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Complete}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{128.280}{602.825}{475.720}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 service until all the other joined federates have successfully completed their restore. In this way, a%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.001}{614.825}{513.999}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 federation manager federate can “throttle” the restore process as it monitors the restore status.%
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
\clearpage

\HLASourcePage{425}{424}
\thispagestyle{hla-body}
\begin{hlafixedpage}
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{69.120}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{12.00}{13.44}{%
 \textbf{E.5 Declaration management (DM)}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{101.363}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{10.98}{12.30}{%
 \textbf{E.5.1 Overview}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{126.786}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 One reason joined federates can subscribe at multiple levels in a class hierarchy is to allow for FOM growth.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{138.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 As a FOM expands, a federate application need not be recoded. It can continue to subscribe at the same%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{150.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 levels in the hierarchy while other, “newer” federate applications in the federation can subscribe at the new%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{162.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 classes. In addition, if a joined federate subscribes to multiple levels in the class hierarchy, it should%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{174.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 generally subscribe to the lowest classes first (and work its way up in the hierarchy) in order to help ensure%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{186.785}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 that object instances are discovered at the most specific type.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{210.784}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Some services in the interface specification contain postconditions that are less precise than one would%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{222.784}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 expect. The postcondition for the %
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{229.499}{222.784}{374.501}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Subscribe Object Class Attributes}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{367.379}{222.784}{236.621}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 service, for example, states only that%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{234.784}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 “the RTI has been informed of the joined federate’s requested subscription.” It does not say that the joined%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{246.784}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 federate is now subscribed to the specified class attributes, nor is a statement to this effect found in any%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{258.784}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 service postcondition.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{282.783}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Services with such imprecise postconditions include the following:%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{306.783}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{---}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.978}{306.783}{482.022}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Subscribe Object Class Attributes}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{318.783}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{---}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.978}{318.783}{482.022}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Subscribe Interaction Class}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{330.782}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 ---%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.979}{330.782}{482.021}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Commit Region Modifications}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{242.518}{330.782}{361.482}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 (old Modify Region)%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{342.782}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{---}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.978}{342.782}{482.022}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Subscribe Object Class Attributes With Regions}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{100.019}{354.782}{503.981}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{---}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{121.978}{354.782}{482.022}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \emph{Subscribe Interaction Class With Regions}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{378.782}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 These services have imprecise postconditions, similar to the postcondition quoted above, because the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{390.781}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 interface specification allows RTI implementations to decide when to determine whether a message sent by%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{402.781}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 one federate should be received by another federate. One possibility is to broadcast all sent messages and%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{414.781}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 perform filtering at each receiving federate. A more efficient possibility is to perform filtering when the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{426.781}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 message is sent (in other words, the message is sent only to interested federates) and again at each receiving%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{438.781}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 federate (in case the state of the receiver has changed during transmission).%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{462.780}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 The problem with allowing the latter alternative is that should the receiver add to its subscriptions, that%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{474.780}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 addition cannot be considered to have fully taken effect until the RTI can ensure that messages sent by any%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{486.780}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 federate will take the added subscriptions into account, thus the imprecise postconditions. Subscription%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{498.780}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 additions take effect locally immediately, but there will be some delay before the additions take effect%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{510.780}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 throughout the federation execution. An RTI implementation makes these changes take effect throughout the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{522.779}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 federation execution as soon as possible, but it will not delay the return of these service invocations until it%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{534.779}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 can guarantee the subscription additions are complete.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{558.779}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Since federates can come and go and can add subscriptions at any wall-clock time, the only way the RTI%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{570.779}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 could meet a postcondition asserting that the federate is now subscribed to the specified attributes would be%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{582.778}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 to synchronize the whole federation execution so that it could make that guarantee. This is one of the places%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{594.778}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 in the specification where the desire was to permit more federate asynchronous behavior. This places more%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{606.778}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 responsibility on the federates to make their subscriptions in sufficient wall-clock time for them to have the%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{618.778}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 desired effect in their federation execution.%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{642.638}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 \textbf{E.5.1.2 Definitions and constraints for object classes and class attributes}%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{666.777}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Item o) in 5.1.2 is a very complex sentence, and it has been suggested that it may be difficult for the reader to%
 }
 \HLAFixedBlock{89.999}{678.777}{514.001}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 parse and understand. This text is an attempt to explain what it means by example.%
 }
\end{hlafixedpage}
\clearpage

\HLASourcePage{426}{425}
\thispagestyle{hla-body}
\begin{hlafixedpage}
 \HLAFixedBlock{90.000}{69.786}{514.000}{\rmfamily\raggedright}{9.96}{11.16}{%
 Consider Figure E.1, the object class inheritance tree.%
 }
